**Definition 2.2 (literal word-program language).** The only register
assignments are empty, copy, literal-bit cons and tail; tail of empty is
empty. Tests are empty and first-bit tests. Sequence, finite case and while
have static finite-flow successors. Internal $n$ is the actual word $1^n$;
wire $U(n)=1^n0$. Define

$$
F(w)=U(|w|)w,\qquad
\mathcal L([])=0,\qquad
\mathcal L(w::r)=1F(w)\mathcal L(r).
$$

A displayed tuple is precisely an $\mathcal L$ list with the displayed
arity. Every enclosing parser requires exhaustion. A symbol is its actual
three-bit code in ARC's order $L,R,0,1,\square,\mathsf{sep},\mathsf{seal},
\mathsf F$; cell tracks are their original eight literal bits. Naturals,
tuple indices, record lengths, paths, stacks and dictionaries are words,
never runtime native addresses or an unbounded finite-control integer.

Reverse, Cat, Length, Equal, Take, ReadUnary, ReadFrame, ReadList, Nth,
Replace and AppendItem have exactly RCA Definition 12.1's acyclic
expansions. For definiteness the additional routines used here are fixed
as follows.

| Routine | Exact word operations and exit |
| --- | --- |
| Prefix$(x,y)$ | Copy both cursors; compare/tail simultaneous leading bits; accept at exhaustion of $x$, reject at mismatch or earlier exhaustion of $y$. |
| LexLess$(x,y)$ | Cancel equal leading bits; proper prefix is less; otherwise 0 is less than 1; equality is false. |
| Find$(D,key)$ | ReadList a copied closed list, compare the designated key by Equal; retain the first item and unary index, else missing. |
| Insert$(D,w)$ | Scan sorted copied $D$, emitting to a separate output; insert before its first greater key, suppress an equal key, append at end if absent. A duplicate-forbidden call rejects equality instead. |
| Push/Pop | Prepend literal 1 and Frame(item) to a word list; Pop is ReadList. No native call stack is used. |
| Emit | Cat of literal 1 and Frame(item) to the output accumulator; append terminal 0 once at end. |
| Add/subtract/compare | Copy unary operands; Cat for addition; tail both for comparison/subtraction, failing subtraction when the right outlasts the left. |
| Half$(1^n)$ | Repeatedly tail a copied cursor twice, consing one mark to the quotient; a leftover single mark is the parity bit. This gives floor/ceiling halves. |
| Fixed-field/cell access | Nth/Replace with a literal index; a dynamic list index is a constructed unary word and uses cancellation. |
| TakeItems$(n,D)$ | Copy unary $n$ and list $D$; once per mark ReadList one whole item and Emit to a different list, tail the mark; fail on early list end, otherwise terminate the output and return the unconsumed list. This counts records, not bits. |
| Finite vector enumeration | Start the list containing the empty pattern; for each location cons each allowed literal symbol to each old pattern into a different list; reverse field order at end. |

Every emitted item is itself built by the earlier word macros. Finite
bits obtained by tests are selected into separate literal branches before
the compiler can overwrite a native read latch. No generic string, sorting,
dictionary, arithmetic, traversal or parser opcode is introduced.

